```redblue
set options to [{name: "count", type: "number"}]
set parsed to sys.parse(options)
for each argument in sys.argv()
    say argument
end
```

`sys.argv()` is the list of arguments after the program path, and it is empty
for a program run from the REPL or from a test, because only `rb run` collects
them. `sys.parse(declaration)` reads those arguments against a list of flag
records and answers a record with one field per flag, plus `arguments`, `help`
and `usage`; `sys.parse(declaration, arguments)` reads an explicit list instead.
A flag has a `name`, a `type` (`text`, `number`, `yes/no` or `path`), and
optionally `takes_value`, `default`, `required` and `help`. A declaration
error, an unknown flag, a missing required flag and a value of the wrong type
are catchable `RuntimeError`s naming the flag, and `--help`/`-h` answers the
generated usage without requiring the rest. The `Cli` module in `modules/`
wraps this: its `run` function prints the usage and skips the body
when help is asked for, and calls `body(parsed)` otherwise; its `run_with`
function takes the argument list explicitly.

### time

```redblue
set stamp to time.now()                  // a record: seconds, nanoseconds
time.sleep(0.5)                          // pause for half a second
say time.format(1704067200, "%Y-%m-%d")  // "2024-01-01"
say time.format(time.date(2024, 2, 9))   // 2024-02-09 00:00:00
say time.parse("2024-01-01T12:00:00+02:00") // 2024-01-01 10:00:00
say time.unix("2024-01-01 00:00:00")     // 1704067200
say time.from_unix(1704067200)           // 2024-01-01 00:00:00
```

`time.now()` is a record of `seconds` and `nanoseconds` since
1970-01-01T00:00:00Z, and `time.sleep` answers `nothing` once the pause is over.
`time.format` takes a date value or a number of seconds since the same instant
and a format string; with no format string it writes `%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S`.
`time.parse` reads an ISO-8601 timestamp as a date, applying any zone offset so
the value is the UTC reading of the instant. `time.parse(text, layout)` reads
# Phase 102 — Let a Redblue program parse its own command line

## What changed

The finding reproduced on the phase base before any edit: `sys` exposed only
`argv`, so `sys.parse([])` failed with
`RuntimeError: Module 'sys' has no function 'parse'`. Every command-line program
had to write the same flag loop and its own usage text.

`sys.parse(declaration)` now reads `sys.argv()` against a list of flag records
and answers a record with one field per flag plus `arguments`, `help` and
`usage`. `sys.parse(declaration, arguments)` reads an explicit list instead.
A flag declaration has `name` and `type` (`text`, `number`, `yes/no` or `path`)
and may add `takes_value`, `default`, `required` and `help`. A `yes/no` flag
defaults to taking no value; every other type takes one. A path flag is wrapped
with phase-100's `path::value`, so `type_of` answers `path` and every filesystem
call accepts it.

The parser accepts `--flag value`, `--flag=value`, repeated flags (last wins),
flags after positionals, `--` to end flag parsing, and an empty string as a
value. An unknown flag, a missing required flag, a value of the wrong type, a
missing value and a malformed declaration are catchable `RuntimeError`s naming
the flag and the expectation. `--help` and `-h` answer the generated usage
without enforcing required flags, including when they stand where a value was
expected. A value-taking flag whose next token is `--` or another declared flag
now reports a missing value naming the flag and its type; it used to swallow
that token as the value and report an unrelated wrong-type failure.

The usage text is generated from the same declaration, in declaration order,
so a flag added to the declaration appears in help without a second edit. The
`modules/Cli.rb` module wraps this for programs: `Cli.run(declaration, body)`
prints help and does not call the body when help was asked for, and
`Cli.run_with(declaration, body, arguments)` takes the argument list explicitly.
The module is written against the language's early-return rule, so help is an
`if`/`else` rather than a `give back` inside the branch.

